.TH BSKYTEST 1
.SH NAME
bskytest \- test the #C/curl/JSON path bsky(1) depends on
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bskytest
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Bskytest
calls
.IR bsky (1)'s
own in-process
.B pubtest()
seam \(em a test-only entry point, the same justification
.BR run() 's
own comment gives for existing at all (see
.IR eqnorbitaltest (1)
and
.IR orbitviewtest (1)
for the same pattern applied elsewhere in this tree).
.B Pubtest
fetches
.BR app.bsky.actor.getProfile ,
a real, public, unauthenticated Bluesky endpoint, through the exact
.B curl()
and JSON-decoding path
.BR bsky (1)'s
own
.B login()
and
.B timeline()
use, and checks that the decoded handle is exactly
.BR bsky.app .
Because the endpoint needs no session, this exercises the whole
transport \(em
.BR #C ,
the host's
.IR curl (1),
real TLS, and this tree's
.B json
library reading the reply back \(em without a credentials file or
any live account.
.PP
.B Postpubtest
calls
.BR login() ,
the one caller of
.BR curl() 's
request-body path and of
.BR jsonquote() ,
with a deliberately bogus handle and password. It cannot reach a real
account, but the real server can only answer with the specific
.B AuthenticationRequired
error shape if the JSON body this sent actually arrived intact \(em a
body mangled by the write/close-to-EOF path would instead produce a
different, distinguishable failure.
.PP
Exits with
.B PASS
or
.B FAIL
naming what the decoded response actually held.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/bskytest.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR bsky (1)
.SH BUGS
Needs real outbound network access to
.BR public.api.bsky.app
and
.BR bsky.social ;
offline or firewalled hosts will see curl itself fail, not a bug in
the code under test. Covers the transport, both request shapes, and
JSON decoding, but not
.BR timeline() 's
own feed-array walk (which needs a real, authenticated timeline to
exercise).
